synopsis: |
  Under the cover of a dead-water slack tide in the port of Piraeus, Kallias—a landless runner and the covert eyes of rising political orator Lysander the Younger—intercepts a midnight parcel smuggled off an unflagged vessel from the Isthmus. The dispatch is supposed to be the final proof needed to ostracize Admiral Phormio at dawn, ridding Athens of an aging commander accused of Spartan sympathies.

  Already suspicious after Lysander ordered him to ignore an unmanifested raft of vital shipbuilding timber rotting on the quays, Kallias ignores the unwritten law of the docks and slices open the wax diptych. Instead of an Athenian mark, he uncovers the seal of a Spartan ephor. The message exposes a catastrophic conspiracy: forty talents of Persian gold are waiting on Delos for Lysander in exchange for banishing Phormio and paralyzing the Athenian fleet under invented timber shortages until winter gales leave the coast defenseless.

  Narrowly escaping Lysander’s armed house-guards in the dark slipways, Kallias realizes that turning the tablet over means his silence will be bought with a slit throat, while throwing it away leaves Athens defenseless. With the gates along the Long Walls monitored, Kallias flees to the military harbor of Zea and seeks out Nikias the Red, a battle-scarred helmsman fiercely loyal to Phormio.

  Seeing the Spartan wax, Nikias mobilizes forty veteran thranitai (top-tier rowers). Rather than starting a hopeless riot in the streets, the oarsmen march through the city gates at dawn armed with ungreased ash sweeps and docking mallets, forming an impenetrable screen around the tribal voting gates in the Agora.

  While the rowers hold Lysander's enforcers at bay, Kallias slips behind the private staging hurdles where Lysander prepares his forged voting shards. Cornering the terrified orator alone with the broken seal, Kallias uses the tablet as lethal blackmail: Lysander must immediately reverse his rhetoric from the mounting block and steer the vote toward a dispensable rival, or the Spartan wax will be delivered directly to the Archon Basileus.

  Lysander yields, taking the podium to desperately clear Phormio's name to the stunned mob. With the fleet secured and the tablet left in Nikias’s hands as a permanent shield, Kallias leaves the dust of Athens behind. Knowing he can never walk the Piraeus road in safety again, he slips aboard a southbound Rhodian merchant vessel—penniless and bound for the open Mediterranean, but finally his own master.
